UNIT hasAnsi
KIND FUNCTIONAL
ORACLE-CLASS deterministic
SIG hasAnsi(input) -> boolean
DEPENDS-ON none   # SELF-CONTAINED: the ansi-regex@3 pattern is INLINED below; the
                  # reconstruction MUST NOT require() any module. See INLINED DEPENDENCY.

# ===========================================================================
# SUMMARY
# ===========================================================================
# hasAnsi(input) returns a boolean: true iff the STRING-COERCED form of `input`
# contains at least one ANSI escape sequence (per the inlined ansi-regex@3
# pattern), false otherwise.
#
# The original CommonJS source (has-ansi@3.0.0, index.js) is, in full:
#
#     'use strict';
#     const ansiRegex = require('ansi-regex');
#     // Remove the `g` flag
#     const re = new RegExp(ansiRegex().source);
#     module.exports = input => re.test(input);
#
# Two load-bearing facts a blind re-emitter MUST reproduce:
#
#  (1) NO TYPE GUARD. The exported function is simply `input => re.test(input)`.
#      It does NOT check typeof input, does NOT throw TypeError on non-strings.
#      RegExp.prototype.test performs ToString(input) on its argument and tests
#      the regex against the coerced string. So non-string inputs are COERCED,
#      not rejected. (See COERCION SEMANTICS + ERROR/THROW below.)
#
#  (2) THE 'g' FLAG IS STRIPPED. ansi-regex@3 returns `new RegExp(pattern, 'g')`
#      (global). has-ansi takes only its `.source` and rebuilds `new RegExp(source)`
#      with NO FLAGS. The comment "// Remove the `g` flag" is exactly this intent.
#      Because the regex used by `.test` has NO global flag, its `lastIndex` never
#      advances and `.test()` is STATELESS / IDEMPOTENT — repeated calls on the
#      same string always return the same boolean. (See STATEFULNESS below.)
#      A blind emitter therefore builds the regex WITHOUT the 'g' flag. Whether
#      the emitter builds it once at module scope or fresh per call is
#      OBSERVATIONALLY IDENTICAL (no 'g' => no lastIndex drift); both are correct.
#
# The unit is a PURE, READ-ONLY predicate. No effects, no ambient reads
# (no clock/RNG/env/fs/net), no mutation. ORACLE-CLASS is plain `deterministic`
# (no seam to inject). Output is a boolean and is fully gradable.

# ===========================================================================
# INLINED DEPENDENCY — the ansi-regex@3.0.1 pattern (verbatim, SELF-CONTAINED)
# ===========================================================================
# has-ansi@3 does `require('ansi-regex')` (resolved to ansi-regex@3.0.1) and uses
# ONLY `ansiRegex().source`. That source is INLINED here so the reconstruction
# needs NO require. The original ansi-regex@3.0.1 (index.js) is, in full
# (each `\\` below is HOW THE SOURCE FILE WRITES one backslash in a JS string
# literal — i.e. the LITERAL .source character is a single backslash):
#
#     'use strict';
#     module.exports = () => {
#       const pattern = [
#         '[\\u001B\\u009B][[\\]()#;?]*(?:(?:(?:(?:;[-a-zA-Z\\d\\/#&.:=?%@~_]+)*|[a-zA-Z\\d]+(?:;[a-zA-Z\\d]*)*)?\\u0007)',
#         '(?:(?:\\d{1,4}(?:;\\d{0,4})*)?[\\dA-PRZcf-ntqry=><~]))'
#       ].join('|');
#       return new RegExp(pattern, 'g');
#     };
#
# has-ansi consumes `ansiRegex().source`, which equals that `parts.join('|')`
# string (the 'g' flag lives on the RegExp object, not the source string; it is
# dropped when has-ansi rebuilds `new RegExp(source)`).
#
# ---------------------------------------------------------------------------
# THE EXACT .source STRING (reproduce CHARACTER-FOR-CHARACTER — #1 divergence risk)
# ---------------------------------------------------------------------------
# CRITICAL TRANSCRIPTION RULE: the regex SOURCE is a 154-character PURE-ASCII
# string. It contains NO raw control bytes. Where an ANSI control byte is meant,
# the .source uses the regex ESCAPE for it as LITERAL TEXT: the 6 characters
# backslash u 0 0 1 B (for ESC U+001B), backslash u 0 0 9 B (for CSI U+009B),
# backslash u 0 0 0 7 (for BEL U+0007). Likewise `\d` is two chars (backslash, d),
# `\/` is two chars (backslash, slash), `\]` is two chars (backslash, ]). There
# are exactly 11 literal backslash characters in the .source. A blind emitter that
# constructs `new RegExp(theString)` MUST give a string whose characters equal the
# .source EXACTLY; one that writes a `/literal/` must make its `.source` equal it.
#
# THE SINGLE CANONICAL FORM — the .source written as a JS SINGLE-QUOTED LITERAL
# (so each `\\` is one backslash in the actual .source). This is byte-verified:
# the literal collapses to a 154-char string === the real ansiRegex().source.
# COPY THIS form to avoid any control-byte transcription hazard. The recommended
# reconstruction is `const re = new RegExp(<this literal>);` (NO flags):
#
#     '[\\u001B\\u009B][[\\]()#;?]*(?:(?:(?:(?:;[-a-zA-Z\\d\\/#&.:=?%@~_]+)*|[a-zA-Z\\d]+(?:;[a-zA-Z\\d]*)*)?\\u0007)|(?:(?:\\d{1,4}(?:;\\d{0,4})*)?[\\dA-PRZcf-ntqry=><~]))'
#
# (If an emitter instead writes a JS /regex literal/, it MUST encode the same
#  characters: \u001B \u009B \u0007 as \u escapes, the literal slash as \/ , and
#  add NO flags so .flags === ''. The safest path is the new RegExp(<literal>) form
#  above — do NOT hand-type raw ESC/CSI/BEL control bytes anywhere.)
#
# ANCHORS (verified by running the real has-ansi@3.0.0 + ansi-regex@3.0.1):
#   - .source.length === 154        (pure ASCII; UTF-8 byte length === 154)
#   - .source contains NO raw control byte; it contains 11 literal '\' characters
#   - parts.join('|') === .source   (the two-part assembly reconstructs it)
#   - .source.startsWith('[\\u001B\\u009B][[')   # begins with the chars:
#         [  \ u 0 0 1 B  \ u 0 0 9 B  ]  [  [
#   - .source.endsWith('*)?[\\dA-PRZcf-ntqry=><~]))')   # ends with the chars:
#         * ) ? [ \ d A - P R Z c f - n t q r y = > < ~ ] ) )
#   - The regex has-ansi builds from it has .flags === '' (NO flags; 'g' removed)
#     and .global === false.
#
# PER-CHARACTER NOTES on the dense classes (described in WORDS to avoid raw bytes;
# every `\u`/`\d`/`\/` referenced is LITERAL TEXT with a single backslash):
#   - Leading class:   the 14 chars   [ \u001B \u009B ]   i.e. bracket, the 6-char
#       escape backslash-u-0-0-1-B, the 6-char escape backslash-u-0-0-9-B, bracket.
#       (ESC U+001B and CSI U+009B, both as \u escapes.)
#   - Next class:      [[\]()#;?]            (literal '[' inside, then \] for ']',
#                                             then ( ) # ; ? ), quantified by '*'
#   - First sub-alt class: [-a-zA-Z\d\/#&.:=?%@~_]   (chars: - a-z A-Z \d / # & . : = ? % @ ~ _)
#   - Second sub-alt class: [a-zA-Z\d]               (ONLY a-z A-Z and digits)   <-- NOTE
#   - BEL terminator:  the 6-char escape backslash-u-0-0-0-7  (for BEL U+0007)
#   - Final class:     [\dA-PRZcf-ntqry=><~]
#       ranges/members: \d , A-P , R , Z , c , f-n , t , q , r , y ,
#                       and the literals = > < ~
#   - Quantifiers used: *  +  ?  {1,4}  {0,4}
#
# ===========================================================================
# !!! VERSION WARNING — DO NOT use the ansi-regex@5 pattern !!!
# ===========================================================================
# A different (longer, 169-char) ansi-regex@5 pattern exists and is WRONG here.
# Distinguishing features of the CORRECT @3 .source you must inline:
#   - @3 second sub-alt class is `[a-zA-Z\d]+(?:;[a-zA-Z\d]*)*`
#       (@5 uses the richer `[-a-zA-Z\d\/#&.:=?%@~_]` there).
#   - @3 final class is `[\dA-PRZcf-ntqry=><~]`  (note `A-PRZ`: range A-P, then R, then Z)
#       (@5 uses `[\dA-PR-TZcf-ntqry=><~]`, i.e. `A-PR-TZ`).
#   - @3 .source.length === 154   (@5 === 169).
# Use the 154-char @3 string above. Nothing else.

# ===========================================================================
# COERCION SEMANTICS — what `re.test(input)` does to a non-string input
# ===========================================================================
# RegExp.prototype.test(x) computes a string S = ToString(x), then matches.
# So the predicate is effectively:  re.test(String(input))  for all coercible
# inputs (with ONE exception: Symbol, see ERROR/THROW). Coercion rules
# (standard JS ToString), all VERIFIED against the real package:
#
#   - string          -> itself
#   - number 42       -> "42"            -> no ANSI    -> false
#   - number 0        -> "0"             -> false
#   - 3.14            -> "3.14"          -> false
#   - true / false    -> "true"/"false"  -> false
#   - null            -> "null"          -> false
#   - undefined       -> "undefined"     -> false
#   - bigint 5n       -> "5"             -> false
#   - {}              -> "[object Object]" -> false
#   - { a: 1 }        -> "[object Object]" -> false
#   - []              -> ""              -> false       (empty -> no ANSI)
#   - [1]             -> "1"             -> false
#   - [ESC + "[31m"]  -> "<ESC>[31m"     -> TRUE         (array of one ANSI string
#                                                         stringifies to that string)
#   - [1,2]           -> "1,2"           -> false
#   - RegExp /re/     -> "/re/"          -> false
#   - object whose toString returns an ANSI string -> that string -> TRUE
#     (ToString uses ToPrimitive hint "string" -> calls .toString())
#
# So: for ANY input, the result is `re.test(String(input))` — true iff the
# coerced string contains an ANSI escape matched by the inlined pattern.
# NONE of the plain numbers/booleans/null/undefined/objects above contain ANSI,
# so they are all false; only inputs whose COERCED string contains an ANSI
# sequence are true.

# ===========================================================================
# STATEFULNESS  (the moving-lastIndex trap — and why it does NOT apply)
# ===========================================================================
# Concern: ansi-regex@3 returns a regex WITH the 'g' flag; a shared global regex's
# `.test()` advances `lastIndex` and yields alternating true/false across calls.
# Resolution: has-ansi DROPS the 'g' flag (`new RegExp(ansiRegex().source)` with
# no flags). A NON-global regex's `.test()` always starts at index 0 and never
# mutates `lastIndex`. Therefore the predicate is STATELESS and IDEMPOTENT.
# EVIDENCE (real has-ansi@3.0.0, in one Node process):
#   - hasAnsi("<ESC>[31mRED<ESC>[39m") called 6x  ->  [true,true,true,true,true,true]
#   - interleaved hasAnsi("<ESC>[31m"), hasAnsi("plain"), repeated
#       -> [true, false, true, false, true]   (each call independent, correct)
#   - the internal regex reports .global === false and .flags === ''.
# IMPLICATION FOR THE EMITTER: do NOT add a 'g' flag. With no 'g' flag it is
# irrelevant whether you hoist the regex to module scope or rebuild it per call;
# both are stable. (The original hoists it once at module scope — equivalent.)

# ===========================================================================
# BEHAVIOR  (ordered pipeline)
# ===========================================================================
# node-1  Construct the matcher regex from the INLINED 154-char .source above,
#         with NO flags (the 'g' is removed). [Build-once or per-call: equivalent.]
# node-2  On call hasAnsi(input): compute S = ToString(input).
#           - For a Symbol input, ToString THROWS a TypeError (node-4); this is
#             V8's coercion throwing, not a guard in has-ansi.
# node-3  Return `regex.test(S)` — a boolean: true iff S contains >=1 ANSI escape
#         matched by the pattern, else false. (No 'g' flag => stateless.)
# node-4  ERROR/THROW: the ONLY throwing inputs are those whose ToString throws —
#         in practice ONLY a Symbol (TypeError "Cannot convert a Symbol value to
#         a string"). There is NO explicit type guard and NO TypeError for plain
#         non-strings (numbers, null, undefined, objects, arrays all COERCE and
#         return false unless their coerced string contains ANSI). Symbols are
#         non-round-trippable through the oracle codec and are NOT emitted by
#         genInputs (documented OUT-OF-ENVELOPE below); if ever exercised the
#         harness records {__throw: TypeError}.

# ===========================================================================
# ORACLE
# ===========================================================================
ORACLE value -> oracle.json
# Output is a primitive boolean. The codec round-trips booleans trivially.
# For each generated input the expected = real-package hasAnsi(input) (true/false).
# No throw paths are generated (Symbol excluded), so all expecteds are booleans.

# ===========================================================================
# SCOPE
# ===========================================================================
# IN-CONTRACT LOGIC (reproduce exactly):
#   - The INLINED 154-char ansi-regex@3 .source as the matcher pattern, byte-exact.
#   - Building the regex with NO flags (the 'g' removed) => stateless predicate.
#   - NO type guard: `regex.test(input)` semantics = `regex.test(String(input))`,
#     i.e. coerce-then-test; non-strings are NOT rejected, they are coerced.
#   - Returning a strict boolean (the value of RegExp.prototype.test).
# CARRIED DATA: none. The pattern is small in-contract code, NOT a carried table.
# OUT-OF-CONTRACT: none (pure, no effects, no stateful API, no ambient seam).
# OUT-OF-ENVELOPE (documented, NOT emitted by genInputs):
#   - Symbol input -> throws TypeError (ToString of a Symbol). Symbols are not
#     codec-faithful; excluded. Behavior documented at node-4.
#   - Functions as input -> coerce via Function.prototype.toString to the source
#     text of the function (e.g. "() => {}") -> almost always false; functions are
#     NOT codec-faithful, so excluded. (Behavior still derivable from coerce-then-test.)
#   - Boxed primitives (new String("...")) -> lose class through the codec; not
#     emitted. (They coerce to their primitive string and behave like that string.)
#   - bare `undefined` / `NaN` / `±Infinity` AS the argument: codec maps these to
#     null on persist (lossy), so they are NOT emitted directly. Their behavior is
#     documented (all -> false) but to keep the persisted arg faithful the generator
#     emits the LITERAL STRINGS "undefined"/"NaN"/"Infinity" instead, which are
#     codec-faithful and which the coerced equivalents test identically (all false).

# ===========================================================================
# SELF-CONSISTENCY CROSS-CHECK (SIR <-> genInputs)
# ===========================================================================
# genInputs emits ONLY single-element tuples [value] where value is one of:
#   - a string (with or without ANSI escapes; ANSI built from the real bytes
#     U+001B (ESC) and U+009B (CSI), NOT from rendered glyphs). For each such string
#     the SIR defines the result as `regex.test(string)` against the inlined
#     pattern — fully specified.
#   - a small set of CODEC-FAITHFUL non-strings whose coercion the SIR defines
#     explicitly above: number, 0, true, false, null, {}, {a:1}, [], [1],
#     [ESC+"[31m"], [1,2], a RegExp, a bigint. Each maps to `regex.test(String(value))`
#     per the COERCION SEMANTICS table (all false except [ESC+"[31m"] -> true).
# Every emitted value's behavior is defined explicitly in the SIR (coerce-then-test
# + the inlined pattern). The generator emits NO value whose behavior is left to an
# undocumented table, and emits NO non-round-trippable value (no Symbol, no function,
# no boxed primitive, no bare undefined/NaN/Infinity argument). The Symbol-throw path
# is documented but NOT generated. SELF-CHECK: PASS.

# ===========================================================================
# EMISSION DIRECTIVE
# ===========================================================================
# The original is `module.exports = input => re.test(input)` (CommonJS default
# export, a single function). Emissions are .mjs consumed via import(). The blind
# re-emitter MUST emit a SINGLE DEFAULT export that is the hasAnsi function:
#     export default hasAnsi
# (exportName=null in the generator tells the harness to call the default export.)
# The emission MUST be SELF-CONTAINED: build the regex from the inlined 154-char
# .source with NO flags; do NOT require()/import any external module.

PROVENANCE has-ansi@3.0.0 (chalk/has-ansi), MIT license, source file index.js;
           INLINED dependency ansi-regex@3.0.1 (chalk/ansi-regex), MIT license,
           source file index.js (pattern .source only; the 'g' flag removed by has-ansi).
